h on oral health varies in design, population, duration, and outcome measures. Randomised trials, observational studies, laboratory evidence, clinical experience, and surveillance data answer different questions. The strongest conclusions come from convergence across suitable methods, while individual decisions still depend on context.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nFrequent free-sugar exposure, tobacco, dry mouth, limited fluoride exposure, ineffective cleaning, some medicines, and barriers to dental care can increase risk. Brushing with fluoride toothpaste, interdental cleaning, and regular assessment are core preventive measures.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nDental pain with facial swelling, fever, difficulty swallowing, uncontrolled bleeding, or trauma affecting breathing or consciousness needs urgent professional attention.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nOral health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Oral health: What evidence can and cannot show","word_count":569,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Oral health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-069","published_at":"2026-04-05 12:00:00+00","slug":"oral-health-how-everyday-choices-affect-outcomes-069","title":"Oral health: How everyday choices affect outcomes","description":"Oral health explained through how everyday choices affect outcomes, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Oral health: How everyday choices affect outcomes\n\nOral health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how everyday choices affect outcomes while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nOral health depends on teeth, gums, saliva, oral microorganisms, diet, hygiene, and access to preventive and restorative care. Dental caries develops when acid produced by bacteria repeatedly demineralises tooth structure, while gum disease involves inflammation around supporting tissues.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How everyday choices affect outcomes\n\nEveryday choices influence health through repeated exposure over time, but they operate alongside genetics, environment, access to care, work, income, and existing disease. For oral health, small consistent behaviours are usually more meaningful than short periods of extreme effort. Frequent free-sugar exposure, tobacco, dry mouth, limited fluoride exposure, ineffective cleaning, some medicines, and barriers to dental care can increase risk. Brushing with fluoride toothpaste, interdental cleaning, and regular assessment are core preventive measures. The aim is not perfection but a pattern that is sustainable and appropriate to the person's circumstances.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nFrequent free-sugar exposure, tobacco, dry mouth, limited fluoride exposure, ineffective cleaning, some medicines, and barriers to dental care can increase risk. Brushing with fluoride toothpaste, interdental cleaning, and regular assessment are core preventive measures.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nDental pain with facial swelling, fever, difficulty swallowing, uncontrolled bleeding, or trauma affecting breathing or consciousness needs urgent professional attention.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nOral health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Oral health: How everyday choices affect outcomes","word_count":598,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Oral health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-070","published_at":"2026-04-06 12:00:00+00","slug":"oral-health-when-professional-care-becomes-important-070","title":"Oral health: When professional care becomes important","description":"Oral health explained through when professional care becomes important, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Oral health: When professional care becomes important\n\nOral health is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on when professional care becomes important while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nOral health depends on teeth, gums, saliva, oral microorganisms, diet, hygiene, and access to preventive and restorative care. Dental caries develops when acid produced by bacteria repeatedly demineralises tooth structure, while gum disease involves inflammation around supporting tissues.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## When professional care becomes important\n\nSelf-care and general health information have limits. Professional care becomes important when symptoms are severe, persistent, progressive, unexplained, or are interfering with normal function. It is also important when a person is pregnant, very young, older and frail, immunocompromised, taking complex medicines, or living with conditions that change risk. Dental pain with facial swelling, fever, difficulty swallowing, uncontrolled bleeding, or trauma affecting breathing or consciousness needs urgent professional attention.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nFrequent free-sugar exposure, tobacco, dry mouth, limited fluoride exposure, ineffective cleaning, some medicines, and barriers to dental care can increase risk. Brushing with fluoride toothpaste, interdental cleaning, and regular assessment are core preventive measures.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nDental pain with facial swelling, fever, difficulty swallowing, uncontrolled bleeding, or trauma affecting breathing or consciousness needs urgent professional attention.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nOral health is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Oral health: When professional care becomes important","word_count":577,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Oral health"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-071","published_at":"2026-04-07 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-how-it-works-071","title":"Physical activity: How it works","description":"Physical activity explained through how it works, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: How it works\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how it works while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How it works\n\nUnderstanding physical activity starts with the underlying system rather than with symptoms alone. Physical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve. A useful explanation therefore separates normal physiology, the factors that shift it, and the signs that suggest the system is no longer coping well.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: How it works","word_count":565,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-072","published_at":"2026-04-08 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-why-prevention-matters-072","title":"Physical activity: Why prevention matters","description":"Physical activity explained through why prevention matters, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: Why prevention matters\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on why prevention matters while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## Why prevention matters\n\nPrevention matters because many health problems develop gradually, and the first opportunity to reduce harm often appears before severe symptoms. In physical activity, prevention can mean reducing exposure to known risks, maintaining protective routines, using recommended preventive services, and acting on early changes instead of waiting for crisis. Age, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: Why prevention matters","word_count":575,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-073","published_at":"2026-04-09 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-what-common-misconceptions-miss-073","title":"Physical activity: What common misconceptions miss","description":"Physical activity explained through what common misconceptions miss, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: What common misconceptions miss\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on what common misconceptions miss while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## What common misconceptions miss\n\nCommon misconceptions usually come from reducing a complex health topic to one rule, one symptom, or one product. Physical activity is not well explained by a single cause in every person. Physical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve. The more useful approach is to distinguish association from cause, screening from diagnosis, and general advice from individual clinical decisions.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: What common misconceptions miss","word_count":582,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-074","published_at":"2026-04-10 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-how-risk-is-assessed-074","title":"Physical activity: How risk is assessed","description":"Physical activity explained through how risk is assessed, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: How risk is assessed\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on how risk is assessed while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## How risk is assessed\n\nRisk assessment combines multiple pieces of information rather than treating one result as destiny. For physical activity, clinicians and public-health professionals may consider age, symptoms, history, exposures, examination findings, trends over time, and tests that answer specific questions. Age, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort. Risk is therefore a probability estimate used to guide next steps, not a guarantee of what will happen.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: How risk is assessed","word_count":586,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-075","published_at":"2026-04-11 12:00:00+00","slug":"physical-activity-what-evidence-can-and-cannot-show-075","title":"Physical activity: What evidence can and cannot show","description":"Physical activity explained through what evidence can and cannot show, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Physical activity: What evidence can and cannot show\n\nPhysical activity is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on what evidence can and cannot show while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nPhysical activity places controlled demand on muscles, bones, the cardiovascular system, metabolism, balance, and the nervous system. Repeated exposure produces adaptations such as improved aerobic capacity, strength, insulin sensitivity, coordination, and functional reserve.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## What evidence can and cannot show\n\nEvidence can clarify average benefits, harms, associations, and likely mechanisms, but it does not erase uncertainty. Research on physical activity varies in design, population, duration, and outcome measures. Randomised trials, observational studies, laboratory evidence, clinical experience, and surveillance data answer different questions. The strongest conclusions come from convergence across suitable methods, while individual decisions still depend on context.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nAge, baseline fitness, disability, chronic illness, recovery, training volume, intensity, technique, and access to safe environments all matter. Benefits come from consistent activity matched to capacity rather than from occasional extreme effort.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew chest pain, fainting, unexplained severe breathlessness, acute neurological symptoms, or a major injury during activity warrants prompt medical evaluation.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPhysical activity is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Physical activity: What evidence can and cannot show","word_count":562,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Physical activity"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text,
  published_at timestamptz,
  slug text,
  title text,
  description text,
  body_markdown text,
  article_type text,
  language text,
  featured_image_url text,
  featured_image_alt text,
  word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer,
  keywords text[],
  provider text,
  model text,
  image_source_type text,
  image_provider text,
  image_model text,
  refs jsonb
);

update public.source_items si
set status='processed',
    rejection_reason=null,
    instructions=coalesce(si.instructions,'{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
      'non_diagnostic_health',true
    )
from _blogdel_health_payload p
where si.external_id=p.external_id
  and si.category_id=(select id from public.categories where slug='health');

update public.articles a
set slug=p.slug,
    title=p.title,
    description=p.description,
    body_markdown=p.body_markdown,
    article_type=p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,
    language=p.language,
    status='published'::public.article_status_enum,
    featured_image_url=p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt=p.featured_image_alt,
    word_count=p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes=p.reading_time_minutes,
    keywords=p.keywords,
    provider='chatgpt',
    model='gpt-5.6-sol',
    is_demo=false,
    event_at=p.published_at,
    published_at=p.published_at,
    archived_at=null,
    generation_job_id=null,
    image_source_type=p.image_source_type,
    image_provider=p.image_provider,
    image_model=p.image_model,
    updated_at=now()
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
where a.source_item_id=si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id, category_id, author_id, generation_job_id,
  slug, title, description, body_markdown, article_type, language,
  status, featured_image_url, featured_image_alt, word_count,
  reading_time_minutes, keywords, provider, model, is_demo,
  event_at, published_at, image_source_type, image_provider, image_model
)
select
  si.id, si.category_id, au.id, null,
  p.slug, p.title, p.description, p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum, p.language,
  'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url, p.featured_image_alt, p.word_count,
  p.reading_time_minutes, p.keywords,
  'chatgpt','gpt-5.6-sol',false,
  p.published_at,p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,p.image_provider,p.image_model
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join lateral (
  select id from public.authors
  where category_id=si.category_id and is_active
  order by last_used_at asc nulls first
  limit 1
) au on true
where not exists (select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_health_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id
  and a.source_item_id=si.id
  and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id, source_item_id, provider, title, url, authority, position, retrieved_at
)
select
  a.id, si.id,
  ref->>'provider', ref->>'title', ref->>'url',
  nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
  coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer, ordinality::integer-1),
  now()
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref,ordinality);

commit;
